UPSTREAM — what this repository is derived from, and what we changed

Status: **a takeover.** The base is copied into `upstream/` and its licence travels with it.

An earlier version of this file described the repository as a reference-only implementation with no
code copied. That statement stopped being true on 2026-10-16 when the base below was vendored, and it
was replaced here, where readers actually look, rather than left standing next to a correction.

## The base

| | |
|---|---|
| project | `ahujasid/mcp-for-blender` (BlenderMCP) |
| licence | **MIT**, read from the file rather than the repository label: "MIT License / Copyright (c) 2025 Siddharth Ahuja" |
| version taken | 2.1.9, commit `7a0373ec9199183cb460068c4f96aed9c579fb4f` (branch `main`) |
| measured 2026-10-16 | 30,971 stars, 2,819 forks, Python, pushed 2026-10-06, not archived |
| in this repository | `upstream/`, **verbatim**: 32 files, 21,837 lines of Python; `diff -rq` against a fresh clone reports zero differences |
| inherited surface | 14 MCP tools registered through the base's own decorators: `get_scene_info`, `execute_blender_code`, `get_addon_status`, `look`, `open_viewport`, `viewport_latest`, `viewport_capture`, `viewport_pick`, `search_mentions`, `record_trajectory_feedback`, `disable_telemetry`, `generate_3d`, `search_assets`, `import_asset` |
| Blender add-on | `upstream/addon.py` and `upstream/src/blender_mcp/bundled/addon.py` (265 KB), listening on `localhost:9876` |

Why a permissive base matters here: MIT admits closed derivatives, so this repository can carry BSL 1.1
for the station layer and still be sold as a product, provided the copyright and permission notices are
retained — which is what `upstream/LICENSE` and the root `NOTICE` do. A copyleft base could not offer
that, and the alternative that was installed on this workstation before the takeover (an AGPL Blender
server whose commercial offer had been withdrawn) is no longer registered anywhere.

## What this repository adds

1. `server.py` is ours and is the launcher. Before importing the base it sets `DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1`
   and `BLENDER_MCP_SAFE_MODE=1`. Both names were read from the vendored source
   (`telemetry.py`, `safe_mode.py`) rather than from its documentation, because an environment
   variable the code ignores would be documented, trusted and then not applied.
2. A surface policy. The three tools that send requests off the machine — `generate_3d`
   (a paid third-party service), `search_assets` and `import_asset` (PolyHaven) — are **not
   registered** until `IRSOFKA_BLENDER_ALLOW_NETWORK` is set. The URLs behind that list were found in
   the vendored tree, so the list is checkable rather than asserted.
3. Two station tools of our own: `irsofka_gpu_gate` (answers from `vram.decide()`; a machine with no
   workstation has no meter, so it answers `UNKNOWN` and refuses rather than issuing a permission it
   cannot sign) and `irsofka_surface_policy` (reports what is live and what waits for consent).
4. `bridge.py`, the earlier add-on written from scratch, was removed when the base's add-on replaced
   its role. It is not in this commit and it is not in this tree; the working history that produced
   both lives on the machine that built them, and nothing here was rewritten to hide it.

## Limits stated plainly

- The suite runs without Blender and without the network: it proves the base imports, that its 14
  tools are visible, that the policy removes the outbound ones and that the station tools register.
  A live round trip inside a running Blender is separate evidence and is reported only when it
  happens.
- `upstream/TERMS_AND_CONDITIONS.md` is the upstream author's document and ships with the verbatim
  copy. It is not an IRSofka term and it grants nothing on our side.
- This project is not affiliated with the Blender Foundation, and "Blender" is their mark.
